1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:04,245
Hi again. Now that you've
learned the key components for

2
00:00:04,245 --> 00:00:07,640
organizing and facilitating
an effective team meeting,

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:09,690
we'll explore the
specifics of some of

4
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:11,625
the most common types of meetings

5
00:00:11,625 --> 00:00:14,100
you'll need to have
during a project.

6
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,235
Every project has meetings,
lots of meetings.

7
00:00:17,235 --> 00:00:19,169
While every meeting is unique,

8
00:00:19,169 --> 00:00:21,300
being familiar with the
most common types of

9
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,130
project management meetings will

10
00:00:23,130 --> 00:00:25,215
help you better
identify the goals,

11
00:00:25,215 --> 00:00:28,440
structure, and activities
best suited for each one.

12
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,090
There are four general types

13
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:32,130
of project management meetings,

14
00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:35,160
project kick-off, status updates,

15
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:38,220
stakeholder reviews,
and project reviews.

16
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,010
The first one we'll talk about

17
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:41,945
is the project kick-off meeting.

18
00:00:41,945 --> 00:00:43,850
This is often considered the

19
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:46,250
official beginning of
a project and serves

20
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,530
as a way to align the
team's understanding of

21
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:51,935
the project goals with
actual plans and procedures.

22
00:00:51,935 --> 00:00:53,300
Members of your team are

23
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,790
the major attendees of
your kickoff meeting.

24
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:57,230
But the participation of

25
00:00:57,230 --> 00:00:59,780
senior management and
key stakeholders is also

26
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:01,700
required for securing buy-in and

27
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:03,835
ensuring alignment
with project goals.

28
00:01:03,835 --> 00:01:05,955
The next type of
meeting we'll discuss,

29
00:01:05,955 --> 00:01:07,640
are status update meetings.

30
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:09,560
Status update meetings are one of

31
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,485
the most common
types of meetings.

32
00:01:11,485 --> 00:01:14,300
This category includes
regular team meetings

33
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,435
where the primary goal is to
align the team on updates,

34
00:01:17,435 --> 00:01:20,330
progress, challenges,
and next steps.

35
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:22,400
During the meeting,
the project manager

36
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:23,660
may distribute or present

37
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:25,760
project performance
reports and formal

38
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,870
status updates on key
elements of the project.

39
00:01:28,870 --> 00:01:31,610
This allows the team and
stakeholders to gain

40
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:33,875
visibility into current
performance levels

41
00:01:33,875 --> 00:01:35,270
and task progress.

42
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:37,745
One of the project manager's
key responsibilities

43
00:01:37,745 --> 00:01:40,370
is to be aware of the
status of the project at

44
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:43,535
any given time and to
ensure that others are

45
00:01:43,535 --> 00:01:47,080
up to speed or know where to
find the latest information.

46
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,455
In order to do that,

47
00:01:48,455 --> 00:01:50,930
status updates become a
critical tool throughout

48
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:53,900
the life cycle of a project
to check in on the project.

49
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,135
Typically, you'll
assess the status of

50
00:01:56,135 --> 00:01:58,750
each of the following
topics during this meeting.

51
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:01,095
First, are task updates.

52
00:02:01,095 --> 00:02:03,800
It is important for
the attendees to know,

53
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,680
what is the status of
the most urgent tasks?

54
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:09,080
How many tasks have
been completed,

55
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:11,315
and how many open items remain?

56
00:02:11,315 --> 00:02:13,690
Next, is schedule status.

57
00:02:13,690 --> 00:02:15,315
Are we behind schedule?

58
00:02:15,315 --> 00:02:18,745
Ahead of schedule? Or on
pace with our projections?

59
00:02:18,745 --> 00:02:21,050
Similarly, it's common to discuss

60
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:22,235
the budget status and

61
00:02:22,235 --> 00:02:25,240
any new items that
impact your bottom line.

62
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:29,150
Then you should discuss
current or anticipated issues.

63
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:31,565
For example, changes, risks,

64
00:02:31,565 --> 00:02:34,700
resource issues, vendor
issues, and so on.

65
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:37,580
Particularly changes
to quality and scope.

66
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,590
It's always a good idea to raise

67
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:41,435
these items on a recurring basis

68
00:02:41,435 --> 00:02:43,250
so that no one is
caught off guard

69
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:45,580
and you can discuss
solutions together.

70
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:48,540
Lastly, you'll want to
discuss action items.

71
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:50,510
An action item is a task on

72
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:52,490
your list that needs
to be completed.

73
00:02:52,490 --> 00:02:55,370
Assigning action items is
a great way to wrap up

74
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:56,630
the meeting and make sure

75
00:02:56,630 --> 00:02:59,080
the project keeps forging ahead.

76
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:04,055
Remember, every action item
has an owner and a due date.

77
00:03:04,055 --> 00:03:06,170
The status meeting is

78
00:03:06,170 --> 00:03:07,700
a fundamental project tool

79
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:09,790
that keeps the project on track.

80
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:12,470
Most project managers
recommend using

81
00:03:12,470 --> 00:03:15,830
a relatively fixed agenda
and time with this meeting.

82
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:17,540
To keep the team engaged,

83
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:18,980
follow the agenda, and hold

84
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:21,025
the meeting to a tight schedule.

85
00:03:21,025 --> 00:03:23,120
Because the project
manager should

86
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:24,830
be able to report up-to-date

87
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:27,080
information to the
project sponsors

88
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:28,700
or clients at any time,

89
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:31,930
it's important to conduct
status meetings regularly.

90
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:34,580
Status meetings are
beneficial because they

91
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:37,655
provide an opportunity for
recognizing milestones,

92
00:03:37,655 --> 00:03:40,960
sharing information, and
raising concerns to the team.

93
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:43,100
How often you decide to schedule

94
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:45,604
these meetings depends
on several factors,

95
00:03:45,604 --> 00:03:47,615
such as the project's complexity,

96
00:03:47,615 --> 00:03:49,100
number of team members,

97
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:51,230
and the level of
information required by

98
00:03:51,230 --> 00:03:54,050
the project sponsor,
clients, or others.

99
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:55,700
Don't be afraid to change up

100
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:58,055
the cadence as your
project progresses.

101
00:03:58,055 --> 00:04:00,515
Another type of project
management meeting,

102
00:04:00,515 --> 00:04:01,955
is a stakeholder meeting.

103
00:04:01,955 --> 00:04:03,860
Stakeholder engagement
is essential

104
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:05,965
for a successful
project management.

105
00:04:05,965 --> 00:04:07,895
The goal of a stakeholder meeting

106
00:04:07,895 --> 00:04:09,785
is to get buy-in and support.

107
00:04:09,785 --> 00:04:11,870
Stakeholders, each
have their own set

108
00:04:11,870 --> 00:04:14,335
of tools, know-how,
and expertise.

109
00:04:14,335 --> 00:04:16,190
Stakeholder meetings are where

110
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:19,115
these contributions are
outlined and utilized.

111
00:04:19,115 --> 00:04:20,975
You'll need to start
by understanding

112
00:04:20,975 --> 00:04:23,255
a stakeholder's
challenges or problems.

113
00:04:23,255 --> 00:04:25,070
Then respond accordingly and make

114
00:04:25,070 --> 00:04:27,895
necessary adjustments to
resolve those challenges.

115
00:04:27,895 --> 00:04:30,290
Winning and sustaining
the support of

116
00:04:30,290 --> 00:04:33,530
your stakeholders is important
to your project's success.

117
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:36,155
In some cases, you
might want to have

118
00:04:36,155 --> 00:04:39,335
stakeholder meetings
on a one-on-one basis.

119
00:04:39,335 --> 00:04:41,510
This allows you to dive deeper on

120
00:04:41,510 --> 00:04:44,005
relevant details with
each stakeholder.

121
00:04:44,005 --> 00:04:46,580
Then you can cover the
topics that are most

122
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:49,685
interesting and concerning
to that particular person.

123
00:04:49,685 --> 00:04:53,105
Other times you'll need to
engage stakeholders in groups.

124
00:04:53,105 --> 00:04:56,165
If you have a large number
of stakeholders to manage,

125
00:04:56,165 --> 00:04:57,470
focus the meeting on

126
00:04:57,470 --> 00:05:00,065
your project's most
influential stakeholders.

127
00:05:00,065 --> 00:05:02,060
Identify appropriate stakeholders

128
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:03,770
for high touch communication.

129
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,440
For example, you may
focus the meeting on

130
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:07,940
senior managers from each of

131
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:09,845
the groups you need to engage.

132
00:05:09,845 --> 00:05:13,205
Other stakeholders can be
informed using other methods,

133
00:05:13,205 --> 00:05:15,605
such as email or meeting notes.

134
00:05:15,605 --> 00:05:17,270
While there are a number of

135
00:05:17,270 --> 00:05:20,255
potential topics to cover when
meeting with stakeholders,

136
00:05:20,255 --> 00:05:21,830
most meetings are limited

137
00:05:21,830 --> 00:05:24,290
to communicating
critical information.

138
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:27,890
You should always be able to
present a project update.

139
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:29,345
Start the meeting with

140
00:05:29,345 --> 00:05:34,355
a short overall project's
status update of 2-5 minutes.

141
00:05:34,355 --> 00:05:36,800
Another key reason to
meet with stakeholders is

142
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:39,230
to seek out and
listen to feedback.

143
00:05:39,230 --> 00:05:41,810
Or you might meet with
stakeholders to make

144
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:42,980
a decision or resolve

145
00:05:42,980 --> 00:05:45,380
a major issue surrounding
your project.

146
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:47,660
In this case, you'll
often meet with

147
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:49,925
senior leaders and
the project sponsor.

148
00:05:49,925 --> 00:05:51,920
Decisions could include go,

149
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,740
or no-go decisions, a
choice between options,

150
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:56,660
or signing off on investments.

151
00:05:56,660 --> 00:05:59,425
Stakeholder meetings are
generally more formal.

152
00:05:59,425 --> 00:06:01,000
It's normal to prepare

153
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,380
reading materials and
documents to review

154
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:05,270
ahead of time to help get people

155
00:06:05,270 --> 00:06:07,520
in the right mindset
for the meeting.

156
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:10,450
Stakeholder meetings can
be regular and recurring,

157
00:06:10,450 --> 00:06:12,880
or just a one-off
project meeting.

158
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:15,320
The frequency will depend on why

159
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:17,360
the stakeholder is
involved in the project.

160
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,430
Are they playing an advisory role

161
00:06:19,430 --> 00:06:21,605
such as a consultant in a RACI,

162
00:06:21,605 --> 00:06:24,955
or just someone to inform
and keep in the loop?

163
00:06:24,955 --> 00:06:26,840
Does their involvement
center around

164
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:28,640
just one activity on the project,

165
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:31,955
or do they need to stay involved
on a longer-term basis?

166
00:06:31,955 --> 00:06:35,500
You'll need to decide on
a case-by-case basis.

167
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,540
The end of the project or the
end of the project phase is

168
00:06:38,540 --> 00:06:39,800
an excellent opportunity to

169
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,085
review how the project unfolded.

170
00:06:42,085 --> 00:06:43,985
This is called a retrospective,

171
00:06:43,985 --> 00:06:45,335
and you'll learn
more about this in

172
00:06:45,335 --> 00:06:46,700
other sections of this course.

173
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:49,325
A typical retrospective
meeting agenda

174
00:06:49,325 --> 00:06:51,635
includes reviewing
lessons learned

175
00:06:51,635 --> 00:06:52,970
about what's going well,

176
00:06:52,970 --> 00:06:54,605
what you should keep doing,

177
00:06:54,605 --> 00:06:56,720
and what can be improved.

178
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:59,630
Equally important to
reviewing lessons learned is

179
00:06:59,630 --> 00:07:00,860
taking the opportunity to

180
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:02,885
celebrate the project's success.

181
00:07:02,885 --> 00:07:04,430
Knowing the difference between

182
00:07:04,430 --> 00:07:06,710
these project meeting
types allows you

183
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:08,420
to maximize productivity and

184
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:10,700
ensure that you're
not wasting time.

185
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:13,400
You'll get the right outcome
for each type of meeting,

186
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:15,320
which will help
drive decisions and

187
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,945
lead to proactive
positive actions.

188
00:07:17,945 --> 00:07:20,900
Amazing, you just learned
about the common types of

189
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:22,580
project meetings and how to make

190
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,940
them effective for
everyone in the room.

191
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:26,555
In the next video,

192
00:07:26,555 --> 00:07:28,490
we'll recap what
you've learned in

193
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:31,710
the past few videos.
I'll meet you there.
